// §3.4.2: `from [0:inf)` admits 0 and everything above it, so -1 is below the
// closed lower bound and the override must be diagnosed. This is the plain
// out-of-`from` case, as opposed to the two `exclude` holes: a compiler could
// implement the holes and still let a value fall off the bottom of the range,
// which is why it is a fixture of its own. The declaration is §3.4.2's own
// worked example, verbatim.
//
// The override is written as an INSTANTIATION. §3.4.2 says "Range checking
// applies to the value of the parameter for the instance and not against the
// default values specified in the device", so the default 0 is legal — legal
// for any compiler, and this file must not ask for a diagnostic on it — and an
// override is the only thing that can state the rule. It cannot be the suite's
// `//! param` directive: that is consumed by the testbench generator, which
// never runs on a reject fixture, so the compiler would be handed the legal
// default alone and the fixture would demand a diagnostic no conforming
// compiler can emit. 78_alias_double_override.va makes the same move for the
// same reason, and Annex C.8 keeps Clause 6 hierarchy in the Verilog-A subset.
//
// The pin used to be the message fragment `range`, because VerA had no
// range-checking diagnostic and so no code to name. E0361 is that code now: the
// value half of §3.4.2, raised on a parameter whose value came from a §6.3
// instance override, which is the only way a value ever reaches a declared range.
// Note also that §3.4.2 puts the error at SIMULATION time; a compiler that aborted
// the run instead of diagnosing the elaborated instance would need the runner
// taught about that verdict.
//
//! lrm 3.4.2
//! lrm C.8
//! reject E0361
module ch3_range_target_below(p, n);
    inout p, n;
    electrical p, n;
    parameter real val3 = 0 from [0:inf) exclude (10:20) exclude (30:40];

    analog I(p, n) <+ val3 * V(p, n);
endmodule

module ch03_parameter_range_below_lower_bound(p, n);
    inout p, n;
    electrical p, n;

    ch3_range_target_below #(.val3(-1)) below(.p(p), .n(n));

    analog I(p, n) <+ 0.0;
endmodule
